Hint files are written in place instead of to a temporary file renamed into
place. The rename is what makes "a hint exists" mean "the hint is complete":
recovery writes a hint-less file's hint before it trims the file's tail, and
a hint that later fails its CRC is rebuilt by a scan that skips the tail
handling, since a file with a hint was sealed. Torn in place by a power cut,
the hint sends the next open to that scan over an untrimmed tail, and
DB::open refuses the database. (The design first expected this to survive.)
It takes a power cut inside the few milliseconds of an open's hint write.
Unfocused runs caught it twice in about 45 minutes; focusing on power cuts
without the latency and stalls that slow opens down caught it less, so it
runs unfocused and is not held to a budget.
Expected: caught (rare).
Guarded-by: "DB recovery: a crash while open writes a hint leaves no hint behind it, and the next open recovers"

